WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.677
Executable path selection should be deliberate.

2
00:00:02.677 --> 00:00:11.854
If lookup behavior is ambiguous, the application may run a different
program than intended because of path configuration, deployment layout, or
inherited environment.

3
00:00:11.854 --> 00:00:19.247
A safer pattern is to choose executables from trusted configuration, known
installation locations, or tightly controlled allowlists.

4
00:00:19.247 --> 00:00:22.943
The goal is to remove guesswork about which program actually starts.

5
00:00:22.943 --> 00:00:26.194
Working directories shape how relative paths are resolved.

6
00:00:26.194 --> 00:00:33.332
If a subprocess inherits a surprising current directory, the same argument
can read or write a different file than reviewers expect.

7
00:00:33.332 --> 00:00:39.004
Sensitive workflows should set the working directory intentionally or
avoid relying on relative paths.

8
00:00:39.004 --> 00:00:47.863
Input and output locations should be built from trusted base directories
using path-aware APIs, with containment checks when local filesystem
access is involved.

9
00:00:47.863 --> 00:00:51.240
Temporary files often connect Python code to child processes.

10
00:00:51.240 --> 00:00:56.658
They may contain uploads, reports, transformed data, extracted content, or
intermediate results.

11
00:00:56.658 --> 00:01:04.624
Predictable names, shared temporary directories, weak permissions,
incomplete cleanup, and concurrent reuse can create exposure or confusion.

12
00:01:04.624 --> 00:01:12.336
Safer designs use platform helpers that create unpredictable names with
appropriate permissions and clean up after both success and failure.

13
00:01:12.336 --> 00:01:15.459
File permissions should match the child process purpose.

14
00:01:15.459 --> 00:01:22.533
A conversion task should not need access to source code, deployment
secrets, unrelated customer files, or broad host directories.

15
00:01:22.533 --> 00:01:26.166
A report generator should write only to approved output locations.

16
00:01:26.166 --> 00:01:37.000
Subprocess security and file handling security are tightly connected
because arguments often point to files, and child processes may trust
those paths without understanding the application policy.
